Why the Affero variant, and not something more permissive.

Quilzo is a thing people run as a service for other people. An ordinary GPL
obligation is triggered by *distributing* software, and nobody distributes a
CMS they are hosting — so under GPL-3.0 a company could take this, run it for
customers, modify it, and never publish a line. The Affero clause closes that:
offering it over a network is the trigger, so anybody running a modified
Quilzo for others has to offer their users the source.

That is the whole reason to pick it. The author is not trying to stop anybody
using this, charging for it, or building a business on it — all of that is
permitted and intended. What it prevents is the one outcome that would make the
project pointless: a closed fork, hosted at scale, with the improvements kept
private and the author's name removed.

